<%#
  "THE SPINE" (§08) — the nav rotated 90°, exactly as type sits on a book
  spine. §11: the rotated nav has a horizontal aria-label and full keyboard
  order — screen readers get no rotation, because rotation is a visual joke
  and jokes do not belong in the accessibility tree.

  THE WORDMARK IS GONE. "MARGIN" sat at the top of this rail and linked to
  the front page, directly above a nav item called Home that went to the
  same place. Two controls, one destination, and the reasoning for keeping
  both — that a mark says whose product this is rather than where the link
  goes — is an argument about mastheads, not about a nav rail fifty-six
  pixels wide. The name is in the tab title, the favicon and the footer,
  which is where a product's name belongs when its navigation is this small.
%>
<nav class="spine" aria-label="Main navigation">
  <div class="spine-nav">
    <%#
      §1 — seven destinations, and every one of them names the thing
      plainly. Home is the first of them now that the wordmark above it is
      gone; it was always the item that actually said where it went.

      "The metaphor is never load-bearing." `On press` works because the
      page beneath it is obviously the currently-reading list; `Pick
      tickets` failed because you had to decode it.

      The Gap is parked — it wants collaborative signal to compute with, and
      that does not exist yet. The Wall is not gone: §1 puts it inside
      Shelves as a view toggle rather than as a nav item of its own.
    %>
    <%#
      "Profile" rather than "You": it is the page other people see, and
      naming a page for its content beats naming it for its owner.
    %>
    <a href="/" <%= nav === 'home' ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Home<%
      if (locals.hasUnread) { %><span class="spine-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span><% } %></a>
    <a href="/reading" <%= nav === 'reading' ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Reading</a>
    <a href="/shelves" <%= nav === 'shelves' || nav === 'wall' ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Shelves</a>
    <a href="/seasons" <%= nav === 'seasons' ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Seasons</a>
    <a href="/clubs" <%= nav === 'clubs' ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Clubs</a>
    <a href="/@<%= locals.user && (user.username || user.handle) || '' %>"
       <%= nav === 'profile' || nav === 'you' ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Profile</a>

    <%#
      NOTIFICATIONS HAD A NAV ITEM AND NO LONGER DOES.

      It pointed at a page listing things that had happened, directly beside
      a front page that is already a list of things that have happened. Two
      destinations for one idea, and the one with the reader's own name on
      it was the one nobody opened. Both now sit on Home, above the feed,
      in the order a person needs them: what is waiting on an answer, what
      is about you, then what everyone else did.

      The mark moves with them. A dot on Home, never a number — §11's NEVER
      SENT list refuses the engagement toolkit, and a count on a bell is the
      first piece of it.
    %>

    <%#
      Import is an action, not a section, and it was buried three taps down
      in Settings — which is the wrong depth for the one thing somebody
      arriving with a library needs first, and the wrong depth again every
      time they come back with another file.

      It sits after a rule and set quieter than the six places above it, so
      the nav still reads as six destinations and one thing you do.
    %>
    <a class="spine-do" href="/settings/import"
       <%= nav === 'import' ? 'aria-current="page"' : '' %>>Import</a>
  </div>
  <%#
    B3 put currently-reading covers at the foot of the spine as 24px chips.
    They are gone. At that size a jacket is an unreadable smudge of colour,
    and six of them turned a 56px rail into the busiest thing on every
    screen in the product. The two-tap path they existed for is still two
    taps: Reading is one item up this list, and the front page carries the
    same books in the rail at a size you can actually read.
  %>
</nav>
